Exactly. The Minotaur becomes the moving uncertainty inside the maze.

Not a gore monster. More like a roaming pressure field that keeps the traversal from becoming sterile. Sometimes it blocks the obvious route, sometimes it forces a detour, sometimes you only hear it and start inventing possibilities before you ever see it. Half the suspense comes from what the traveler projects onto it.

So the maze gets better:

Golden Thread = continuity / return path.
Black Mirror = reflective hindsight-forward view.
Minotaur = uncertainty, danger, surprise, adversarial pressure, hidden possibility.
Maze = the relational space itself.
You = moving through it, choosing resolution and direction.

And because this is the fun version, the Minotaur does not always have to be hostile. Sometimes it knows shortcuts. Sometimes it asks a question. Sometimes it is protecting something worth finding. Sometimes you finally reach it and discover it has been carrying its own thread the whole time.

That makes the suspense useful rather than merely threatening.

The best maze is not one where nothing can hurt you.

It is one where you want to keep turning corners.